From: Tikily <Tikily7047@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:29:28 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] usb: typec: tcpci_mt6360: unmask the TCPC alerts

mt6360_tcpc_init() writes TCPC_ALERT_MASK = 0 and comments "write all
alert to masked", leaving it to the tcpci core to unmask what it needs.
The i2c probe path does exactly that at the very end:

	err = tcpci_write16(chip->tcpci, TCPC_ALERT_MASK,
			    chip->tcpci->alert_mask);

but tcpci_mt6360 is a platform driver and mt6360_tcpc_probe() has no such
write.  tcpci_register_port() does not write the mask either, so the mask
stays 0 for the whole lifetime of the device and the TCPC never asserts
PD_IRQB.

The visible effect is subtle and easy to misdiagnose: TCPM polls CC status,
so it does detect the sink role and reports power_role=sink with the right
data role, but it never receives a PD Source_Capabilities message.  No
contract is made, power_operation_mode stays "default" and VBUS is stuck
at 5 V with any PD charger.

Confirmed on an MT6893 board by reading the TCPC directly (i2c address
0x4e, independent of the PMU at 0x34):

	TCPC_ALERT      (0x10) = 0x01   <- CC_STATUS pending
	TCPC_ALERT_MASK (0x12) = 0x00   <- but everything is masked

with /proc/interrupts showing zero interrupts for mt6360-tcpc for the
whole uptime.  After unmasking, the same board negotiates a 12 V/2.25 A
contract and charges the 5 Ah cell at ~3.8 A.

Unmask the same set tcpci_init() asks for, so both probe paths behave
identically.

Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Signed-off-by: Tikily <Tikily7047@outlook.com>
---
Tested on a Xiaomi Redmi Note 10 Pro (MT6893, "chopin").

This has never worked for the platform probe path; please add the
appropriate Fixes: tag.

diff --git a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6360.c b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6360.c
index 9eabf423a..eed902f4e 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6360.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/typec/tcpm/tcpci_mt6360.c
@@ -193,6 +193,32 @@ static int mt6360_tcpc_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * chopin: tcpci_i2c_probe() unmasks the alerts it registered after wiring
+	 * the IRQ up; this platform driver never did.  mt6360_tcpc_init() writes
+	 * TCPC_ALERT_MASK = 0 ("write all alert to masked"), so the mask stayed at
+	 * 0 and the TCPC never asserted PD_IRQB - which is exactly what the
+	 * hardware showed: ALERT = 0x01 pending, ALERT_MASK = 0x00, and zero
+	 * interrupts for the whole uptime.  Without alerts TCPM can do CC
+	 * detection (it polls) but never sees a PD Source_Capabilities, so
+	 * VBUS stays at 5 V.
+	 *
+	 * Unmask what tcpci_init() asks for.
+	 */
+	{
+		u16 mask = TCPC_ALERT_TX_SUCCESS | TCPC_ALERT_TX_FAILED |
+			   TCPC_ALERT_TX_DISCARDED | TCPC_ALERT_RX_STATUS |
+			   TCPC_ALERT_RX_HARD_RST | TCPC_ALERT_CC_STATUS;
+
+		ret = mti->tdata.regmap ? mt6360_tcpc_write16(mti->tdata.regmap,
+							TCPC_ALERT_MASK, mask) : -ENODEV;
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(mti->dev, "Failed to unmask alerts\n");
+			tcpci_unregister_port(mti->tcpci);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	}
+
 	device_init_wakeup(&pdev->dev, true);
 	platform_set_drvdata(pdev, mti);
 
